---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeFromFile from '../../../components/CodeFromFile.astro';
const lesson = '/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum/basic/bright-spark/unit-02-give-a-choice-a-signal/';
const samples = 'sinclair-zx-spectrum/basic/bright-spark/opening/';
const breadcrumbs = [{label: 'Bright Spark', href: '/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum/basic/bright-spark'}, {label: 'Find the wrong note'}];
---
<Layout title="Bright Spark: find the wrong note" description="An optional investigation: predict, observe and repair one missing pitch assignment in a separate copy of Bright Spark." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <article class="page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Bright Spark · Optional investigation · Spectrum 48K BASIC</p>
      <h1>Find the wrong note</h1>
      <p class="lead">The panels light in the right order, but one note is wrong.
        We’ll find where its value first differs from our expectation.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="reading-layout"><div class="reading-body">
      <p>Start after <a href={lesson}>Give a choice a signal</a>. We need its
        variables, <code>IF</code> conditions and nested <code>GO SUB</code> calls.
        We do not need to identify notes by ear: a temporary number display gives
        us another way to investigate.</p>
      <h2>Keep a working copy</h2>
      <p>Save the working unit 02 program first. Use a separate emulator session,
        or clear BASIC with <code>NEW</code> (A at the command prompt, then Enter)
        before entering the complete example below. Adding it over the working
        program would leave an old line in memory and hide our fault. Do not save
        this experiment over <code>spark</code>.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Deliberately broken program — investigation only</summary>
        <CodeFromFile src={samples + 'experiments/wrong-note/broken.bas'} />
      </details>
      <h2>Predict, then investigate</h2>
      <p>Run with no keys held. Each run starts by drawing four resting panels,
        then requests panels 1, 2, 3 and 4. We expect pitches 0, 4, 7 and 12,
        each lasting 0.15 seconds, with each asterisk disappearing afterwards.</p>
      <p>Before running, trace one call from line 310 through 900 and 500 to 920.
        Write down the four pitch values we think will actually reach{' '}
        <code>BEEP</code>. Run it and describe expected and actual behaviour.
        Which parts still work?</p>
      <p>Here is the broken run captured from the 48K PAL emulator. Listening is
        optional; a correct-looking panel is not proof of a correct pitch.</p>
      <audio controls preload="none" aria-label="Bright Spark deliberately broken four-cue run" src="/audio/bright-spark/wrong-note.wav"></audio>
      <p>Choose a specific hypothesis before editing: is the wrong value already
        in <code>note</code> when line 920 begins, or does it change during sound
        playback? Predict a value that would support the first explanation and
        one that would contradict it.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Hint 1 — where could we observe the value?</summary>
        <p>Line 910 calls the renderer before line 920 sounds the note. Add this
          temporary observation between them:</p>
        <CodeFromFile src={samples + 'experiments/wrong-note/observe.bas'} />
        <p>Run again. Row 21 now shows one number per panel, from left to right.
          Compare them with 0, 4, 7 and 12. This extra printing costs time; use it
          to inspect values, not to judge the original rhythm.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Hint 2 — which assignments run?</summary>
        <p>Follow line 540 and the conditions after it with <code>p=3</code>.
          Which assignment supplies the default note? Which instruction should
          replace that value for this panel? A previous panel's value does not
          survive an unconditional assignment.</p>
      </details>
      <p>Make the smallest repair we can justify. Before opening the explanation,
        say why it should fix the faulty panel without changing the other three.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Repair and explanation — open after investigating</summary>
        <p>The diagnostic row reads 0, 4, 0, 12. Panel 3 is the first mismatch:
          we expected 7, but it already contains 0 before <code>BEEP</code>.
          Line 540 resets <code>note</code> to zero on every renderer call.
          The special assignment for panel 3 is missing. It therefore uses the
          default, rather than retaining panel 2's pitch 4.</p>
        <CodeFromFile src={samples + 'experiments/wrong-note/repair.bas'} />
        <p>Run with the observation still present: the row should now read
          0, 4, 7, 12. Check all four panels, not only the repaired one.
          Changing the default at line 540 would also change panel 1; altering{' '}
          <code>BEEP</code> would obscure the missing selection rule.</p>
      </details>
      <h2>Check the repair and put the tools away</h2>
      <p>Delete the temporary line by entering <code>915</code> on its own and
        pressing Enter. Run twice. Each run should show the original order,
        four rising pitches and no remaining asterisk. Compare the program with
        the complete working checkpoint in <a href={lesson}>unit 02</a>: after
        repair and removal of the observation, they should match.</p>
      <p>Explain which observation separated our two hypotheses and why checking
        only the picture would have missed this fault. Then return to the saved
        working program. The experiment has not changed the version we keep.</p>
    </div></div>
  </article>
</Layout>
<style>
  .page { max-width: 850px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.5rem 4rem; }
  p { font-family: var(--font-family-read); line-height: 1.7; }
  h1, h2 { line-height: 1.25; }
  h2 { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; }
  :global(.page pre) { overflow-x: auto; padding: 1rem; background: var(--color-surface); }
  audio { max-width: 100%; }
  details { margin-block: 1.5rem; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; }
</style>
